window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][22] = "<p><strong>Decision.</strong> As documented, university officials asked a statistician to analyze the data, and the analysis was done at the level of departments and published in Science on 7 February 1975, concluding that the aggregate pattern was &quot;clear but misleading&quot; and that, properly pooled, there was a small but statistically significant bias in favor of women <code>[V: Bickel et al., Science 187, 398 to 404, abstract]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> Reported: the study concluded that the aggregate pattern reflected where women applied and that departmental decisions showed few significant departures and a small bias toward women when pooled correctly. The case became a standard teaching example of Simpson's paradox. Whether any specific policy changed as a result is not established in the sources used here.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> <em>Inference.</em> Had the analysis been done only on the aggregate, a policy aimed at departments could have been unjustified. Had the department-level data shown consistent disadvantage to women, the aggregate gap would have been explained differently and the response would have been to examine those decisions. The method to carry away: when a rate differs between two groups, break it down by the factor that decides the outcome and compare the mix. For a PM this is the check before any story about a metric: did the rates change within the groups, or did the groups change?</p>\n<span id=\"c12-11-interview-questions-and-model-answers\"></span>\n<h3>11. Interview questions and model answers</h3>\n<p>Seven questions: two Beginner, two Intermediate, three Hard. Model answers are <code>Illustrative</code>; every figure is an assumption, and the arithmetic was computed. Each is written against the six behaviors in section 1. None is tied to a company: statistics questions appear at all of them, and the company lenses in section 7 describe how the emphasis differs. The answers are fuller than a first pass in a real round; a spoken version keeps the same order with fewer words.</p>\n<span id=\"iq-c12-01\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C12-01 · Beginner · analytics · AN</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Average session length rose 10%, but the median fell. What might be going on?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The data must be wrong,&quot; or &quot;users are engaging more.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Explains that a mean can rise while the median falls when the distribution is skewed, and says what to check.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Gives a small numeric example, names several causes (a few very long sessions, a logging or definition change, a shift in the user mix), says which summary answers which decision, and proposes the first check.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> Both can be true, and the gap between them is the clue. The mean is pulled by the tail, and the median describes the typical session, so the typical session got shorter while a few long ones got longer.</p>\n<p>A small example makes it concrete. Ten sessions before: nine of 3 minutes and one of 13. The mean is (27 + 13) ÷ 10 = 4.0 minutes and the median is 3. After: nine of 2.5 minutes and one of 21.5. The mean is (22.5 + 21.5) ÷ 10 = 4.4 minutes, up 10%, and the median is 2.5, down. The typical person spent less time, and one heavy session grew.</p>\n<p><em>What could cause it.</em> A few sessions got very long: a tab left open, a bot, a video that plays unattended, or a heavy user using a new feature. The definition of a session changed (for example, a longer idle timeout merges visits into one long session). The mix of users shifted: more casual users with short sessions and the same few power users. Or a real change: a new feature helps some people go deeper and makes others leave sooner.</p>\n<p><em>What I'd check first.</em> The distribution itself: a histogram and the 25th, 50th, 75th and 95th percentiles before and after. Then whether the session definition or logging changed, whether bots or idle sessions are included, and whether the mix of new and returning users changed. I'd break the number down by segment (C12.7) to see if the pattern is a mix effect.</p>\n<p><em>Which number to use.</em> It depends on the decision. If the question is &quot;is the typical user better off?&quot;, the median and the share of sessions above a useful length. If it is &quot;how many total hours are we delivering?&quot;, the mean, since totals are sums. I'd report both with the group sizes, and I would not call a 10% rise in the average an improvement until I know which of these it is.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What is the first check you would run? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Whether the measurement changed. A new session timeout, a logging change or a new platform can move the mean and median without any change in behavior. Then I would look at the distribution: percentiles (25th, 50th, 90th) and a histogram for before and after, and the mix of new and returning users and of platforms. If the percentiles show the middle falling and the top rising, the typical session got shorter and a few got longer. I would report both numbers and say that the median describes the typical experience and the mean describes total time.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Which number answers whether users spent more time? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;It depends on the decision. For capacity and cost, total time matters, which is the mean times the number of sessions. For whether the typical user is getting more value, the median and the share of sessions above a threshold matter. Here the mean rose and the median fell, so total time may be up while the typical session is shorter, which could mean the product became less engaging for most users while a few heavy users carried the average. I would say that plainly and not choose the more flattering number.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c12-02\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C12-02 · Beginner · analytics · AN</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Explain a 95% confidence interval to a non-technical executive.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;It's the range where the true value is 95% likely to be,&quot; or a technical definition the executive cannot use.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Gives a plain statement with an example, explains what it says about uncertainty, and links it to a decision.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Avoids the common misreading, uses counts, ties the interval to a decision threshold, and says how more data would narrow it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'd say it in terms of the decision. &quot;We measured how many customers buy, in a sample of 10,000: 4.0% bought. We don't know the true rate for all customers, because we only saw some. The confidence interval is the range of true rates that are consistent with what we saw. Here it runs from about 3.6% to 4.4%. So when someone says 'conversion is 4%,' what the data really says is 'somewhere around 3.6% to 4.4%'.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Why the width is what it is.</em> &quot;The more customers we measure, the narrower the range. To make the range half as wide, we need about four times as many customers. So a bigger sample buys precision, and it buys it slowly.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>How to use it.</em> &quot;If we need conversion to be at least 4.5% to justify the project, then the whole range is below the bar, so we should not expect to clear it. If the bar were 3.5%, the whole range is above it, and we can be fairly confident. If the bar were 4.0%, the range straddles it, and we don't know yet.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>What it does not mean.</em> &quot;It is not the range that 95% of customers fall in. Individual customers either buy or not. And I'd be careful with the phrase '95% chance the true value is inside': the careful statement is that if we repeated the whole measurement many times, about 95% of the ranges we'd produce would contain the true value. For a decision, the plain version above is close enough.&quot;</p>\n<p>I'd close with the one thing the executive should take away: ask for the range, not only the number.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What is the common misreading, and how do you avoid it? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;The common misreading is that there is a 95% chance the true value lies in this particular interval. The careful statement is that the method produces intervals that contain the true value 95% of the time. For an executive I avoid probability language and say the interval is the range of true rates that are consistent with what we saw. I tie it to a decision: if the break-even rate is 3.5% and the interval is 3.6% to 4.4%, the product clears the bar even at the low end; if the break-even were 4.2%, the data would not settle it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would more data change the interval? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;It narrows with the square root of the sample. With 10,000 customers and a 4% rate the interval is about ±0.38 points; quadrupling the sample to 40,000 halves it to about ±0.19 points. So a lot more data is needed for modest precision, and I would ask what precision the decision requires before collecting more. If the decision is the same anywhere between 3.6% and 4.4%, more data is not worth the cost.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c12-03\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C12-03 · Intermediate · analytics · AN</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> An A/B test shows conversion up 2% (relative) with p = 0.04. Do you ship?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, it's significant,&quot; or &quot;no, 2% is too small,&quot; without reasoning.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks for the absolute change, the sample size and the interval, and compares with the cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Sets the decision threshold from the cost, reads the interval against it, checks the test's validity (sample ratio, peeking, multiple metrics), and gives a decision with the condition that would change it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'd not answer from the p-value alone. I'd ask five things.</p>\n<p><em>The numbers.</em> Suppose the base rate is 5.00% and the variant is 5.10%, which is a 2% relative and 0.1 point absolute lift. With 400,000 users in each arm, p comes out near 0.04. The 95% interval for the lift is about +0.004 to +0.196 points, or from nothing to about four percent in relative terms.</p>\n<p><em>What it is worth.</em> Suppose 10,000,000 visitors a month and $10 of contribution per extra purchase (invented). The best estimate, +0.1 point, is 10,000 purchases and about $100,000 a month. The interval runs from about $4,000 to about $196,000 a month.</p>\n<p><em>The threshold.</em> Suppose the change costs $50,000 to build and $5,000 a month to maintain (invented). The upkeep is covered if the lift is at least 0.005 points, which is 500 purchases a month. The lower end of the interval, 0.004 points, is just under that, and the best estimate is twenty times it. So the interval mostly clears the cost and the bottom edge touches it.</p>\n<p><em>Validity.</em> I'd check that the arms are the planned size (a mismatch can signal an assignment problem, covered in C14), that nobody stopped the test the first time it crossed the threshold, and that the 2% was the one metric chosen in advance, not the best of several. If we looked at five metrics and report the best, a p of 0.04 is much weaker.</p>\n<p><em>The decision.</em> &quot;I would ship it, with a guardrail on a metric the change could harm, because the whole interval is above zero, most of it is above the cost, and the change is cheap and reversible. I'd tell the team the gain is probably real and could be small, and that I wouldn't plan the roadmap around +$100,000 a month. I'd change my mind if the check showed the test was not run as planned, or if the maintenance cost were several times higher, in which case the interval would straddle break-even and I'd extend the test.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What validity checks would you run before trusting the test? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;First, whether the split matches the plan: with a 50/50 design, check that the arms have about equal numbers (a sample ratio mismatch suggests a bug). Second, how the test was monitored: was it stopped early when the result looked good? Third, how many metrics were examined: with 20 metrics at the 5% level, about one will look significant by chance, so I would want the primary metric named in advance. Fourth, duration: whole weeks to cover weekday patterns, and a check for a novelty effect. Fifth, whether the lift is consistent across major segments.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What would change your decision? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;The value and the cost of shipping. With 10 million visitors a month, a 0.1-point lift is 10,000 extra conversions; at the $10 of contribution used above that is $100,000 a month, and at the low end of the interval (0.004 points) about $4,000 a month. If shipping is cheap and reversible, I would ship to a staged group with a 5% holdout for four weeks to confirm. If shipping adds maintenance cost or risk, I would ask for a replication first. Either way, I would write the decision rule before the next read.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c12-04\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C12-04 · Intermediate · analytics · AN</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Roughly how many users do you need to detect a one-point lift on a 10% conversion rate, and how long would that take?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A few thousand,&quot; or &quot;as many as possible.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Uses a rule or formula, states the assumptions (power, significance), and converts to duration.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Shows what happens at half the lift, states that the rule is approximate, uses whole weeks, and gives options if the test is too long.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'll use the rule of 16, which I'd say is a sketch: for 80% power at the usual 5% significance, users per arm ≈ 16 × p(1 − p) ÷ δ². Here p = 0.10, so p(1 − p) = 0.09, and δ = 0.01. That is 16 × 0.09 ÷ 0.0001 = 14,400 per arm, so about 28,800 in total. An exact calculation gives about 14,750 per arm, so the rule is within a few percent.</p>\n<p><em>Duration.</em> Assume 4,000 eligible users a day, split evenly: 2,000 per arm per day. 14,400 ÷ 2,000 = 7.2 days. I'd run two full weeks, not eight days, so that each weekday appears in both arms equally and any weekly cycle averages out.</p>\n<p><em>If the lift were half as large.</em> For δ = 0.5 points the requirement is 16 × 0.09 ÷ 0.005² = 57,600 per arm: four times as many for half the lift. That is 28.8 days at the same traffic, so five whole weeks. This is why the smallest effect worth detecting should come from the decision, not the calendar.</p>\n<p><em>If the test is too long.</em> I'd raise the minimum effect if the decision allows it, narrow the population to people the change targets, measure a metric closer to the change so there is less noise, or accept lower power and release in stages with a guardrail. I'd not set the effect size to fit the traffic and then report whatever comes out.</p>\n<p><em>Caveats.</em> The rule assumes independent users, a two-arm test on a proportion, and that I have chosen the effect and the duration before starting. If users interact (a social feature) or if I will look at the data repeatedly, a data scientist should design it.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if you want to detect a lift of half a point instead? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;The sample grows with the inverse square of the lift, so halving the lift quadruples it: n per arm = 16 × 0.09 ÷ 0.005² = 57,600, about 115,000 in total. At 4,000 eligible users a day that is about 29 days, so I would plan five whole weeks. This is why the minimum effect worth detecting should come from the business value of the lift, not from what is convenient: a test powered for a one-point lift can miss a half-point effect that is still worth shipping.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the test would take too long? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;There are options. Test a bolder change with a larger effect. Choose a more sensitive metric or reduce noise, for example with a pre-experiment covariate adjustment <code>[V: Deng, Xu, Kohavi and Walker, CUPED, WSDM 2013, pp. 123 to 132: pre-experiment data to reduce metric variance]</code>. Increase the eligible traffic. Accept lower power and a wider interval and say so. Or decide on cost and reversibility without waiting for significance. I would rather state what the test can and cannot detect than run a test whose conclusion is a foregone shrug.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c12-05\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C12-05 · Hard · analytics · AN</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> An account-takeover detector catches 95% of takeovers and wrongly flags 3% of legitimate logins. Takeovers are 0.2% of logins. What should happen when the detector flags a login?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Lock the account, since the detector is 95% accurate.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Computes the share of flags that are real with counts, and sees that most flags are false alarms.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Matches the response to the cost of each error (a challenge is cheap, a lock is not), shows how the number moves with the false-alarm rate and the base rate, and sets metrics for both kinds of error.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'd build the counts for 100,000 logins.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Takeovers: 0.2% of 100,000 is 200. The detector flags 95% of them: 190.</li>\n<li>Legitimate logins: 99,800. The detector flags 3%: 2,994.</li>\n<li>Total flags: 190 + 2,994 = 3,184. Of these, 190 are real.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The chance that a flagged login is a takeover is 190 ÷ 3,184, about 6%. Roughly 94 flags in 100 are false alarms. &quot;95% accurate&quot; is the catch rate among takeovers, a different number from the share of flags that are right.</p>\n<p><em>What follows for the response.</em> Locking the account on a flag would lock 2,994 legitimate users for every 190 attackers, which is harmful and expensive. A cheap challenge (a code sent to the user's phone) costs the legitimate user a few seconds and stops most attackers. So the response should match the cost of a wrong call: a challenge for a flag, and a lock only when the evidence is much stronger (more signals, or a failed challenge).</p>\n<p><em>How the number moves.</em> If the false-alarm rate fell from 3% to 0.5%, legitimate flags would be about 499 and the share of flags that are real would rise to 190 ÷ 689, about 28%. If the base rate fell (attacks drop) with the same detector, the share of real flags falls too, so the precision must be recomputed over time and not assumed fixed.</p>\n<p><em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of takeovers stopped. Guardrails: the share of legitimate logins challenged (here about 3%), challenge failure rate among legitimate users, and support contacts about lockouts. Counter metric: the time attackers spend inside an account before detection, which a looser threshold would shorten. I'd set the threshold with the security and support teams, and I'd say which cost is higher in this product, since the right balance differs by what the account holds.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would the 6% change with a lower false-alarm rate? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;A lot, because precision depends on the base rate and the false alarms together. With 100,000 logins and 190 caught takeovers: at a 3% false-alarm rate there are 2,994 false flags and 190 ÷ 3,184 is about 6% precision. At 1% there are 998 false flags and precision is 190 ÷ 1,188, about 16%. At 0.5% it is 499 false flags and 190 ÷ 689, about 28%. Improving the false-alarm rate matters more than the catch rate here, because takeovers are rare.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What response matches the cost of each error? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;A tiered response. A flag triggers a cheap, reversible action, such as a step-up verification, since wrongly challenging a real user costs a few seconds. Locking the account needs more evidence, such as a new device plus a password change plus a high-risk action. The metrics are takeovers prevented, the share of legitimate users challenged and the false-lock rate, with a guardrail on login completion. I would review the thresholds when the base rate changes, because a campaign of attacks changes the precision of the same detector.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c12-06\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C12-06 · Hard · analytics · AN</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Average order value fell from $24.00 to $21.82 after a marketing push, but it rose in every country. What happened and what do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;It's an error,&quot; or &quot;the marketing push hurt order value.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Identifies a mix shift (Simpson's paradox) and computes a like-for-like comparison.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Notices that the shift itself (fewer orders from the high-value country) is the real question, reports a mix-adjusted figure and the revenue effect, and proposes where to look.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> This is Simpson's paradox: a rate that rises within every group and falls overall because the mix of groups changed. I'd confirm with the numbers (invented).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Before: orders</th>\n<th>Before: average value</th>\n<th>After: orders</th>\n<th>After: average value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Country X</td>\n<td>1,000</td>\n<td>$60</td>\n<td>200</td>\n<td>$62</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Country Y</td>\n<td>9,000</td>\n<td>$20</td>\n<td>9,800</td>\n<td>$21</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>All</td>\n<td>10,000</td>\n<td>$24.00</td>\n<td>10,000</td>\n<td>$21.82</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Before, revenue was 1,000 × $60 + 9,000 × $20 = $240,000, an average of $24.00. After, it was 200 × $62 + 9,800 × $21 = $12,400 + $205,800 = $218,200, an average of $21.82. Order value rose in X ($60 to $62) and in Y ($20 to $21). It fell overall because X, the high-value country, went from 10% of orders to 2%.</p>\n<p><em>A like-for-like figure.</em> At the old mix (10% X, 90% Y) and the new averages: 0.1 × $62 + 0.9 × $21 = $6.20 + $18.90 = $25.10, against $24.00 before, up about 4.6%. Within each country things improved.</p>\n<p><em>The real question.</em> The headline average is not the main problem. What matters is that orders from X fell from 1,000 to 200, and total revenue fell from $240,000 to $218,200 (about 9%) on the same 10,000 orders. The push brought more orders in Y and X shrank. I'd ask why: did the campaign pull spend and attention away from X, was there an outage, a regulatory problem, a competitor or a shipping change in X, or did the push change who is counted in X? I'd look at X's funnel before and after, by week, to find when it dropped.</p>\n<p><em>What I'd report.</em> Order value by country and a mix-adjusted figure, with order counts, and revenue in total. I'd not tell leadership that the push &quot;lowered order value,&quot; since within each country it rose, and I'd not say it succeeded, since total revenue fell. The decision depends on the unit economics in each country (margin per order, cost to acquire) and on whether the shift toward Y is intended. I'd change my view if X's drop were found to be unrelated to the push, in which case two separate stories sit in one number.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you report a mix-adjusted figure? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By holding the mix fixed. Using the before-period mix of 10% from country X and 90% from country Y with the after-period values of $62 and $21 gives 0.10 × 62 + 0.90 × 21 = $25.10, against $24.00 before, an increase of about 4.6%. That is the like-for-like change, and it shows the average order value rose within countries. I would report both numbers and say which question each answers: the unadjusted figure answers what an average order is worth now, the adjusted one answers whether orders got more valuable.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What do you do about the mix shift itself? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I treat it as the real finding. Orders from country X fell from 1,000 to 200, and revenue fell from $240,000 to $218,200, about 9%, even though total orders were unchanged. I would find out why: the campaign may have reached country Y, supply or pricing in X may have changed, or X customers may have left. Then decide whether the push is a success: it brought orders from lower-value customers and lost higher-value ones. The decision depends on margins by country, so I would ask for contribution, not revenue, before changing the campaign.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c12-07\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C12-07 · Hard · analytics · AN</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Your data scientist says the test is underpowered and recommends running six more weeks. The launch is in two weeks. What do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Launch anyway,&quot; or &quot;wait six weeks,&quot; without weighing the stakes.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Describes what the current data does and does not say, weighs the cost of delay against the cost of being wrong, and offers options.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies both sides, distinguishes reversible from irreversible decisions, proposes a staged rollout with a stopping rule and a long-run holdout, and keeps the data scientist as a partner.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'd treat it as a decision under uncertainty, not as a fight between the test and the date. First I'd ask the data scientist for what the data says now: the estimate and its interval, and what lift the test was designed to detect. Suppose the lift is +0.3 points with an interval of −0.5 to +1.1, and the team's threshold for worthwhile is +0.5.</p>\n<p><em>What the data says.</em> &quot;Underpowered&quot; means the interval is wide: it includes harm, nothing, and a gain above the threshold. I can't say the change works, and I can't say it doesn't.</p>\n<p><em>The two costs.</em> Delay: if the true lift is +0.5 points on 6,000,000 monthly visitors at $10 per purchase (invented), the change is worth about 30,000 purchases, $300,000 a month, so six weeks' delay costs roughly $420,000 of benefit if the change is good. Being wrong: if the true effect is −0.5 points the change costs about $300,000 a month, and the question is how fast we would see it and undo it.</p>\n<p><em>Reversibility is the deciding fact (C02).</em> If the change can be undone in a day, the cost of being wrong is limited to the days it runs, and the cost of delay is real. If it cannot be undone (a migration, a public commitment), I'd wait or reduce the risk differently.</p>\n<p><em>Options.</em> (1) Launch in stages: 10% of users, then 50%, then all, with a guardrail and a written stopping rule (stop if the guardrail metric drops by more than a set amount), while the test continues in the background with a holdout group that never gets the change, so the full six weeks of data arrive anyway. (2) Narrow the metric or population so the test is more sensitive, for example the first-step completion rate among new users, and check whether the shorter test is now adequate. (3) Raise the minimum effect: if the change only pays above +0.5 points and the data cannot rule out a smaller effect, say that the question is whether the team would act on a result of +0.3. (4) Delay six weeks, if the decision is hard to reverse.</p>\n<p><em>My recommendation.</em> If reversible: option 1 with the holdout. It keeps the date, caps the downside and still produces the evidence. I'd say to leadership: &quot;We don't yet know if this helps. We'll release in steps with a stop rule, and we'll know by [date]. If it hurts, we'll pull it within a day.&quot; I'd thank the data scientist for raising the issue and ask them to own the stopping rule. I'd change my mind if the guardrail could not be measured quickly, in which case staging gives no protection and delay is safer.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you stage the rollout and what stopping rule would you use? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Ramp to 5%, then 25%, then 100% over the two weeks, checking guardrails at each step. The stopping rule is written in advance: stop the ramp if the lower end of the interval for the guardrail metric falls below a limit (for example, a conversion loss worse than 0.5 points) or error rates exceed twice the baseline. I would keep a 5% holdout for the six weeks the data scientist asked for, so that the original test finishes and we get the answer after launch. The launch is a feature flag, so it is reversible, which is why shipping with a holdout is reasonable.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you keep the data scientist as a partner? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By treating their warning as a requirement for the plan. I would ask them to define the stopping rule and the holdout design, document the assumptions together, and commit to reading the full result in six weeks and to reversing if it turns negative. I would present the decision to leadership as ours, with the data scientist's uncertainty stated plainly, and credit them for the guardrails. If I ignored the warning, I would lose their trust and the next test would be less honest.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c12-12-exercises\"></span>\n<h3>12. Exercises</h3>\n<p>Ten exercises. Each has a model solution and an alternative. Figures are assumptions unless marked, and the arithmetic was computed.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c12-01\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C12-01 · Which summary?</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Ten order values in dollars: 12, 14, 15, 15, 16, 18, 19, 22, 25, 400. Compute the mean, the median and the 90th percentile (nearest rank). Which would you report to (a) forecast revenue, (b) describe a typical customer?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> One very large order is in the data.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Three minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Use the summary selector (FW-C12-02).</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Sum = 556, so the mean is $55.60. The median is the average of the 5th and 6th values, (16 + 18) ÷ 2 = $17. The 90th percentile by nearest rank is the 9th value, $25. (a) For a revenue forecast, the mean (or the total, $556), since revenue is a sum and the large order is part of it, though one large order is unlikely to repeat, so I'd also look at the total without it ($156, mean $17.33). (b) For a typical customer, the median, $17. The mean describes almost nobody.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Report the median with the share of orders above $25 (10%), and the total, so each reader gets the number that fits their question.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A forecast built on the mean is fragile when a single order dominates it; say so.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c12-02\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C12-02 · An interval by hand</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> 400 of 5,000 users clicked an email link. Compute the click rate and an approximate 95% interval. How many users would give a margin of ±0.5 points?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> You will report the click rate to a team that wants one number.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Standard error √(p(1 − p) ÷ n); interval ± 1.96 standard errors (C12.3).</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> p = 400 ÷ 5,000 = 8.0%. Standard error = √(0.08 × 0.92 ÷ 5,000) = √0.00001472 ≈ 0.00384, or 0.38 points. The margin is 1.96 × 0.38 ≈ 0.75 points, so the interval is about 7.25% to 8.75%. For a margin of ±0.5 points, n = (1.96 ÷ 0.005)² × 0.08 × 0.92 = 153,664 × 0.0736 ≈ 11,300 users, about 2.25 times the current sample for two thirds of the margin.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Use the shortcut margin ≈ 2 × √(p(1 − p) ÷ n) = 2 × 0.00384 = 0.77 points: close to the exact figure.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> The formula assumes independent users and a sample that represents the population; an email list of the most engaged users would bias the rate upward.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c12-03\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C12-03 · Read the interval</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A test gives a lift of +0.3 points with a 95% interval of −0.4 to +1.0. The smallest worthwhile effect is +0.5 points. What do you conclude, and what would narrow the interval?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The test ran as planned.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Three minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Compare the whole interval with zero and with the threshold (FW-C12-03).</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> The interval includes zero (so it is not significant at 5%), includes harm down to −0.4, and includes the threshold: values from −0.4 to +1.0 are all consistent with the data, and +0.5 sits in the middle. The conclusion is &quot;we don't know yet,&quot; not &quot;no effect.&quot; To halve the width (from about 1.4 points to 0.7), we would need about four times the data.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Decide on cost and reversibility: if the change is cheap and reversible, a staged rollout with a guardrail may be reasonable while the data accumulate.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Four times the data may be too slow; a more sensitive metric might narrow the interval faster.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c12-04\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C12-04 · Size a test</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Baseline conversion 5%. You want to detect +0.5 points. 3,000 eligible users arrive each day, split evenly. How many users per arm, how many weeks, and what if you only need +1 point?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Use the rule of 16.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C12-05.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> p(1 − p) = 0.05 × 0.95 = 0.0475. For δ = 0.005: 16 × 0.0475 ÷ 0.000025 = 30,400 per arm (the exact figure is about 31,200). At 1,500 per arm per day, 30,400 ÷ 1,500 = 20.3 days, so three whole weeks. For δ = 0.01: 16 × 0.0475 ÷ 0.0001 = 7,600 per arm, 5.1 days, so one whole week. Halving the effect quadruples the sample: 20.3 days against 5.1, or three whole weeks against one after rounding.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Test a more sensitive upstream metric (for example, clicks on the new element) to detect a smaller change faster, and say that it is a proxy.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A one-week test misses weekly cycles beyond one full cycle; for a purchase that varies by weekday, one full week is the minimum.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c12-05\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C12-05 · Triage four results</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> The smallest worthwhile lift is +1.0 point. Classify each result (lift, 95% interval) and say what to do: (a) +2.5, [+1.6, +3.4]; (b) +0.15, [+0.05, +0.25]; (c) +0.05, [−0.15, +0.25]; (d) +0.8, [−0.9, +2.5].</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> All tests ran as planned.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Use the significance triage (FW-C12-04).</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) Significant and big: the whole interval is above +1.0; ship and check guardrails. (b) Significant but small: the interval excludes zero and sits wholly below the threshold; do not ship unless it is nearly free. (c) Not significant and precise: the interval is narrow around zero and below the threshold; conclude there is no worthwhile effect and stop. (d) Not significant and imprecise: the interval includes harm, zero, and values above the threshold; we don't know. Get more data, or release in stages if the change is cheap and reversible.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> For (b), if the change costs nothing to keep and carries no risk, shipping can still be right, because a real small gain is free.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> The threshold must be set before the test; set afterward, it can be bent to fit the result.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c12-06\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C12-06 · A base-rate calculation</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A quiz flags &quot;likely power users.&quot; 10% of users are power users. The quiz identifies 60% of power users and wrongly flags 15% of other users. Of 1,000 users, how many are flagged, and what share of flags are power users? If the perk offered to each flagged user costs $20, what is the cost per true power user reached?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The team plans to give the perk to every flagged user.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Natural frequencies (FW-C12-06).</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Power users: 100; flagged: 60. Others: 900; wrongly flagged: 135. Total flagged: 195. The share of flags that are power users is 60 ÷ 195 ≈ 31%. Cost: 195 × $20 = $3,900, which reaches 60 power users, $65 each. 69% of the spend goes to people who are not power users.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Raise the bar (a stricter quiz) to cut false flags, or give a cheaper perk to flagged users and a larger one only after a second signal.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A stricter quiz misses more power users; the right bar depends on what a missed power user and a wasted perk each cost.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c12-07\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C12-07 · Find the reversal</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A product compares guided setup with self-setup by completion rate. Small accounts: guided 90 of 100, self 800 of 1,000. Large accounts: guided 600 of 1,000, self 40 of 100. Compute each rate, the overall rates, and explain the result.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A manager says self-setup is better because its overall rate is higher.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The trap detector (FW-C12-07): break down by the factor that decides the outcome.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Small accounts: guided 90%, self 80%. Large accounts: guided 60%, self 40%. Overall: guided (90 + 600) ÷ (100 + 1,000) = 690 ÷ 1,100 = 62.7%; self (800 + 40) ÷ (1,000 + 100) = 840 ÷ 1,100 = 76.4%. Guided is better in both groups and worse overall because guided was used mostly for large accounts (1,000 of 1,100, or 91%), which complete less often, while self-setup was used mostly for small ones (1,000 of 1,100). The overall comparison is between different mixes. The manager's conclusion is wrong; compare within the account size.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Standardize both to the same mix (for example 50% small and 50% large): guided = 0.5 × 90% + 0.5 × 60% = 75%, self = 0.5 × 80% + 0.5 × 40% = 60%.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Guided setup was probably given to harder cases on purpose, so even the within-group comparison may be confounded; a randomized test is stronger (C14).</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c12-08\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C12-08 · Twenty slices</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A team slices an experiment result by 20 user segments and finds one with p = 0.03. What is the chance of at least one such result if nothing is going on, and what do you do with it?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The overall result was not significant.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> 20 segments, a 5% threshold.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Four minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Multiple comparisons (C12.7).</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> With a 5% threshold and 20 independent comparisons, the chance of at least one false positive is 1 − 0.95²⁰ = 64%. The expected number of false positives is 20 × 0.05 = 1, so finding one is what chance alone predicts. A simple correction is a threshold of 0.05 ÷ 20 = 0.0025, which p = 0.03 does not meet. Treat the segment as a hypothesis, state a reason it would respond differently, and test it in a new experiment aimed at that segment, with the hypothesis written in advance.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> If a plausible mechanism was specified before looking, the segment is a planned comparison and deserves a stronger look, though still a confirmation.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A strict correction misses real small effects; a retest costs time but is cheap against launching on a false segment.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c12-09\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C12-09 · Did the email work?</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A team emailed the 10% of users with the lowest activity last week (average 1.0 sessions). This week their average rose to 1.4, a 40% increase. The team concludes the email works. What is wrong, and how would you test it?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> No control group was kept.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Regression to the mean (C12.7).</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> The group was selected for an extreme low value, partly by chance, so it would tend to move toward the average even with no email. To separate the effect, take the lowest-activity users, randomly split them in two, email half and not the other, and compare the changes. Suppose the control group also rose, from 1.0 to 1.3 (invented). Then the email's effect is about 1.4 − 1.3 = 0.1 sessions, not 0.4, and most of the 40% rise was regression.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> If a control group is not possible, select a comparison group by the same rule from an earlier week, and compare how that group moved without any email.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Withholding the email from half the group costs a little reach and buys a credible answer.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c12-10\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C12-10 · Critique a dashboard</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Critique these five dashboard items and say what you would ask for: (a) a bar chart of weekly signups whose vertical axis starts at 9,500; (b) a line chart with signups on the left axis and revenue on the right axis, showing lines that move together; (c) a cumulative chart of total users that keeps rising; (d) &quot;average rating 4.6&quot; with no count; (e) a table of conversion rates by country without denominators.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> You are reviewing a weekly business report.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The chart reading checklist (FW-C12-08).</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) A truncated axis exaggerates small changes; ask for a zero baseline or label the range, and show the percentage change. (b) Two axes let the author choose scales so that the lines look related; ask for the correlation with the data, and for each series on its own chart or indexed to 100. (c) A cumulative total rises even when growth has stopped; ask for the weekly additions. (d) A rating without a count could rest on 12 reviews or 12,000; ask for the number and the distribution of ratings. (e) Conversion rates without denominators hide small samples; ask for the visits and conversions and an interval for the smallest countries.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Pick the two most likely to mislead a decision this week and ask only those two questions, which keeps the meeting moving.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Skepticism toward a chart should not feel like an attack on the author; frame each point as a question about the data.</p>\n<span id=\"c12-13-practice-protocol\"></span>\n<h3>13. Practice protocol</h3>\n<p><strong>Timed drills (three a week for four weeks).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Week 1: summaries and intervals.</em> Use IQ-C12-01, IQ-C12-02, EX-C12-01, EX-C12-02 and EX-C12-03. Say the answer aloud in plain language with one numeric example. Ten minutes each.</li>\n<li><em>Week 2: tests, power and sample size.</em> Use IQ-C12-03, IQ-C12-04, EX-C12-04 and EX-C12-05. Do the arithmetic without a calculator using the rule of 16 and the interval rules, then check with a calculator.</li>\n<li><em>Week 3: base rates and traps.</em> Use IQ-C12-05, IQ-C12-06, EX-C12-06, EX-C12-07, EX-C12-08 and EX-C12-09. Build the counts table every time. Name the trap and the check.</li>\n<li><em>Week 4: decisions under uncertainty.</em> Use IQ-C12-07 and EX-C12-10. Run two timed answers to prompts you have not seen, with a partner who uses the interventions in section 4.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Explain it to someone.</strong> Each week, explain one idea (a p-value, an interval, a base rate) to a friend outside product, in three sentences with one example. If they cannot repeat it back, the explanation is not ready.</p>\n<p><strong>Arithmetic drill.</strong> Practice without a calculator: square roots of round numbers, the margin for n = 100, 400, 1,000 and 10,000, and the sample size for 10%, 5% and 20% baselines at 1-point and 0.5-point lifts. After a week, these should take ten seconds each.</p>\n<p><strong>Feedback checklist (yes, partly, no).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Did I fix what is compared, the unit and the window first?</li>\n<li>Did I ask for the sample size and the interval?</li>\n<li>Did I choose a summary that fits the shape of the data?</li>\n<li>Did I read the result against a decision threshold, not only against significance?</li>\n<li>Did I build a counts table for any flag or detector?</li>\n<li>Did I name the trap that could be at work and the check?</li>\n<li>Did I state my arithmetic assumptions and say the rules are approximate?</li>\n<li>Did I explain it in plain words with one example?</li>\n<li>Did I give a decision and what would change it?</li>\n<li>Did I finish with the two-sentence close from section 4?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer journal.</strong> For each drill record the date, the prompt, the arithmetic, the trap you named, your score out of ten and the step you rushed. After eight drills, look for the step you skip most (usually the threshold or the base rate) and drill it alone for a week.</p>\n<span id=\"c12-14-implementation-playbook\"></span>\n<h3>14. Implementation playbook</h3>\n<p>Apply the chapter on real work over the next three weeks. Each deliverable doubles as an interview story.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 1 to 3.</strong> Take the most recent experiment result or metric report your team used for a decision. Run the six-question check (FW-C12-01) on it. Write down what was missing (the interval, the sample size, the threshold, the mix) and what it would have changed.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> For the main metric you own, compute a rough interval with the shortcut in C12.9 and write the metric as &quot;value, with a range.&quot; Share it with your manager. Ask whether the last change in the metric was larger than the range.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Check one metric that is reported as an average for skew. Ask the data team for the median and the 90th percentile, and write what each says that the average does not.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Size one upcoming test with the rule of 16 (FW-C12-05). Write the smallest effect that would change the decision, the duration in whole weeks and the options if it is too long. Compare your estimate with the data scientist's.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Take one alert, flag or model in your product and build the counts table (FW-C12-06) from its hit rate, false-alarm rate and base rate. Write the share of flags that are right and what response fits it.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 3.</strong> Break a recent change in a rate down by the main segments (device, country, new versus returning) and compare the mix before and after (FW-C12-07). Write whether a mix shift explains any of the movement.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverables that become stories:</strong> the six-question review (a time you found a gap in an analysis before a decision was made), the sample-size sketch (a time you set the minimum effect from the decision, not the traffic), and the counts table (a time you showed a team that most flags were false alarms and changed the response). Keep the numbers real, and keep what you did separate from what the team did.</p>\n<span id=\"c12-15-key-takeaways-and-further-reading\"></span>\n<h3>15. Key takeaways and further reading</h3>\n<span id=\"c12-key-takeaways\"></span>\n<h4>Key takeaways</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Treat every number as an estimate with a range: ask how many, how noisy, compared with what, and whether the size matters, before asking whether it is significant.</li>\n<li>Choose the summary that fits the shape of the data and the decision: the median for a typical user, the mean for a total, a high percentile for the worst common experience.</li>\n<li>Read the p-value, the interval and the effect size together, against a threshold set from the cost of the decision; &quot;significant&quot; and &quot;worth doing&quot; are different questions, and &quot;not significant&quot; with a wide interval means &quot;unknown.&quot;</li>\n<li>Apply the base rate with counts before trusting a flag, a test or a correlation, and match the response to the cost of each kind of error.</li>\n<li>Name the trap and the check: a mix shift (Simpson's paradox), survivorship, regression to the mean, and many comparisons; and say when you would bring in a data scientist.</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c12-further-reading\"></span>\n<h4>Further reading</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Ron Kohavi, Diane Tang and Ya Xu, <em>Trustworthy Online Controlled Experiments</em> (2020). The standard practical text on running and reading experiments in products.</li>\n<li>Ronald L. Wasserstein and Nicole A. Lazar, &quot;The ASA's Statement on p-Values: Context, Process, and Purpose,&quot; <em>The American Statistician</em> 70(2), 129 to 133 (2016). A short authoritative statement of what p-values do and do not say <code>[V: The American Statistician 70(2), 129 to 133, doi 10.1080/00031305.2016.1154108]</code>.</li>\n<li>Gerd Gigerenzer, <em>Calculated Risks</em> (2002), published in the United Kingdom as <em>Reckoning with Risk</em>. Natural frequencies and how to teach risk <code>[V: Calculated Risks: Simon &amp; Schuster, 2002, ISBN 9780743205566 (US title); published in the UK as Reckoning with Risk, Penguin, 2002]</code>.</li>\n<li>Darrell Huff, <em>How to Lie with Statistics</em> (1954). An old and still useful guide to misleading charts and averages.</li>\n<li>Daniel Kahneman, <em>Thinking, Fast and Slow</em> (2011). The chapters on the law of small numbers and regression to the mean.</li>\n<li>Judea Pearl and Dana Mackenzie, <em>The Book of Why</em> (2018). Simpson's paradox and causal reasoning.</li>\n<li>P. J. Bickel, E. A. Hammel and J. W. O'Connell, &quot;Sex Bias in Graduate Admissions: Data from Berkeley,&quot; <em>Science</em> 187(4175), 398 to 404, 7 February 1975 (doi 10.1126/science.187.4175.398). The primary source for CS-C12-02.</li>\n<li>Andrew Gelman and Eric Loken, &quot;The Garden of Forking Paths&quot; (2013, unpublished manuscript). The idea behind the mental model of unrecorded analysis choices <code>[V: unpublished manuscript, Columbia Department of Statistics, 14 November 2013]</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c13\"></span>\n<h2>C13 — Diagnosing a Metric Change</h2>\n<span id=\"c13-1-what-interviewers-test\"></span>\n<h3>1. What interviewers test</h3>\n<p>&quot;Daily active users dropped 5% yesterday. What do you do?&quot; is among the most common analytical questions in product loops, and it is the question candidates most often answer badly. The usual answer is a list: a bug, a competitor, seasonality, a bad release, maybe a holiday. The interviewer then asks, &quot;How would you know which?&quot; and the answer ends. They are not looking for the longest list of causes. They are looking for a sequence: what you would check first, why, what each check would show, and how you would get from a confusing number to a cause and a decision.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it shows up.</strong> <code>Inferred</code> from the round structures candidates commonly report: metric-drop and metric-rise questions appear in analytical or execution rounds at Google, Meta, Microsoft, Amazon, Apple, Netflix and Stripe, often in the second half of a loop <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. On the job it is the work of the PM on call for a metric: someone notices a line moving, and the PM has to decide whether it is real, what caused it, and what to do before the next meeting.</p>\n<p><strong>What is scored.</strong> Whether your answer is ordered, quantitative and decision-oriented <code>Inferred</code>. Interviewers listen for whether you first confirm the change is real, whether you check cheap and common causes before exotic ones, whether you use segments to locate the change, whether you state what each hypothesis predicts so that it can be tested, and whether you finish with a decision and a communication. A candidate who says &quot;I'd segment by platform, then region&quot; without saying what they expect to see, or what they would do if they saw it, has described a method and not a diagnosis.</p>\n<p>Six observable behaviors:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You clarify the metric, the size, the timing and the baseline, and confirm the change is real before explaining it.</li>\n<li>You work through causes in a stated order, cheapest and most common first.</li>\n<li>You locate the change with segments and funnel steps, and quantify how much each explains.</li>\n<li>Each hypothesis predicts something else you can check, and you rule causes in or out rather than collecting them.</li>\n<li>You decide (fix, roll back, pause, monitor or accept) with the reversibility and cost of waiting in view.</li>\n<li>You say what you would tell stakeholders and how you would stop it recurring.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The failure this prevents.</strong> Six patterns end diagnosis answers: a brainstorm with no order, treating the first plausible story as the answer, never checking whether the data is complete or the definition changed, slicing by everything until something looks odd, explaining the direction but not the size, and stopping at &quot;found the cause&quot; with no decision or communication.</p>\n<span id=\"c13-2-the-concept-from-first-principles\"></span>\n<h3>2. The concept from first principles</h3>\n<span id=\"c13-c13-1-what-diagnosis-is\"></span>\n<h4>C13.1 What diagnosis is</h4>\n<p><strong>Metric diagnosis</strong> is a structured investigation into why a metric changed, ending in a cause, a decision and a communication. It has three outputs, and an answer is incomplete without all three: <em>what happened</em> (the change, located), <em>why</em> (the cause, with evidence), and <em>what to do</em> (a decision and a message). Brainstorming possible causes is the start of the work and is usually the weakest part of it.</p>\n<p><strong>The stance.</strong> Treat the question as detective work with a budget: you have a limited time, many suspects and cheap tests. The skill is ordering the tests so that each one removes the most uncertainty for the least effort, and stopping when the evidence explains the change in timing, location and size.</p>\n<p><strong>Three tests of an explanation.</strong> A cause explains a change only if it matches on three things: <em>timing</em> (the change began when the cause did), <em>location</em> (it is concentrated in the segments the cause would touch) and <em>size</em> (the cause is big enough to produce the change). A candidate cause that matches on direction alone is a guess.</p>\n<p><strong>When not to use the full sequence.</strong> If the movement is inside the normal range of day-to-day variation (C12's noise band), the right response is to monitor, and a long investigation is itself a cost. If the change is the planned consequence of a decision (an integrity cleanup, a pricing change), the work is to check it was the size you expected, not to hunt for a cause. If the question is about a future change, you are in experiments (C14).</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> Asked about a 5% drop in daily active users, a candidate says in the first two minutes: &quot;It could be a bug, a competitor launch, seasonality, a bad release, a change in the algorithm, an outage, or user fatigue. I'd say it's probably a competitor.&quot; The interviewer asks how the candidate would tell a bug from a competitor. The candidate has no test, no order and no data in mind: the list has no way to arrive at an answer, and &quot;probably a competitor&quot; is the cause least likely to be true on a sudden drop and hardest to check. A candidate who instead says &quot;First I'd confirm the drop is real and complete, then check whether anything shipped in the hour it started, then see which platform or country it sits in&quot; has proposed three cheap tests that remove most suspects.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Hidden risks.</strong> Two. <em>Fixation:</em> the first explanation becomes the frame, and later evidence is bent to fit. Counter it by writing down what each hypothesis predicts and by seeking the observation that would refute your favorite. <em>False precision:</em> naming one cause for a change that has several. Real changes are often a mixture (a small release effect on top of a holiday on top of a mix shift), and the honest answer says how much each explains.</p>\n<span id=\"c13-c13-2-answer-architecture-and-time-budget\"></span>\n<h4>C13.2 Answer architecture and time budget</h4>\n<p>Section 4 sets out the full sequence with a time budget: clarify and confirm, locate, hypothesize, test and rule out, decide, communicate and prevent. It adds the cause taxonomy and the decomposition tools in this chapter to the structuring habits of C03 and the statistical judgment of C12.</p>\n<span id=\"c13-c13-3-step-zero-clarify-the-metric-size-and-timing-confirm-t\"></span>\n<h4>C13.3 Step zero: clarify the metric, size and timing; confirm the change is real</h4>\n<p>Before explaining a change, make sure there is one. FW-C13-02 is a card of questions to ask in the first two minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>The metric.</strong> What exactly is measured, per what unit (users, accounts, sessions), over what window, and has its definition changed recently? A change in definition looks like a change in behavior.</p>\n<p><strong>The size.</strong> In absolute and relative terms. A 5% drop in 10,000,000 daily users is half a million people; the same percentage in a metric of 2,000 users could be a handful.</p>\n<p><strong>The shape.</strong> A step (one level to another, on a date), a slide (gradual decline), a spike (one-off) or an oscillation. A step points to an event (a release, an outage, a policy); a slide points to a mix or behavior change; a spike points to an event that passed or to abuse.</p>\n<p><strong>The baseline.</strong> A <strong>baseline</strong> is the value a metric would be expected to take without the event under investigation, estimated from recent history and the same period in earlier cycles. Compare the new value with the baseline, not with yesterday. Two corrections matter. <strong>Seasonality</strong> is a regular, calendar-linked pattern in a metric (day of week, holidays, school terms, weather) that recurs and should be removed before inferring cause: compare Tuesday with earlier Tuesdays, and a holiday week with last year's holiday week. And the noise band from C12: how big are normal swings? If daily orders usually move by about 2.5% either way, a 3% dip is unremarkable and a 6% one is not.</p>\n<p><strong>The completeness of the data.</strong> The most recent period is often incomplete. Events arrive late (a mobile app that uploads logs when the phone next connects, a pipeline that finishes hours after midnight), so a dashboard read in the morning can show yesterday or today as sharply down for no reason except lag. Compare partial days with the same hours on earlier days, or wait for the data to settle.</p>\n<p><strong>A second source.</strong> Does another measure agree? If daily active users fell and sessions per user, revenue and support tickets did not move, the fall may be in the measurement.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Many &quot;drops&quot; are lag, definition changes or noise, and ruling them out in two minutes saves an investigation. <strong>When not to belabor it.</strong> If the interviewer says &quot;assume the drop is real and the data is complete,&quot; accept it and move on, and say that you would have checked.</p>\n<span id=\"c13-c13-4-taxonomy-of-causes-data-and-instrumentation-product-ch\"></span>\n<h4>C13.4 Taxonomy of causes: data and instrumentation, product change, external, mix shift, abuse, real change in behavior</h4>\n<p>A taxonomy turns &quot;what could it be?&quot; into a checklist that is <a href=\"#gl-mece\">MECE</a> enough to work through (C03). FW-C13-03 sets it out with the tell for each, the cheap check, and who usually owns the answer. Work through it in order, cheapest and most common first.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Family</th>\n<th>What it looks like</th>\n<th>Cheap check</th>\n<th>Usual owner</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Data and instrumentation</td>\n<td>A step change with no matching change in related metrics; one platform or event affected; lag; a duplicated or dropped event; a changed definition</td>\n<td>Compare with a second source; check late-arriving data; ask what changed in logging or the pipeline</td>\n<td>Data engineering</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Product change</td>\n<td>The change begins within hours of a release, an experiment, a configuration change, a campaign or a notification change; concentrated in the affected surface or version</td>\n<td>Check the release log, flags and experiments; plot the metric by app version and by the hour of rollout</td>\n<td>Product and engineering</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. External</td>\n<td>A pattern that matches a holiday, a season, the weather, a news event, a competitor action, an app-store or operating-system change, a partner outage</td>\n<td>Compare with the same period last year; check status pages and news; look at whether similar products moved</td>\n<td>PM, with marketing and partnerships</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4. Mix shift</td>\n<td>The overall metric moves though behavior within groups does not; the share of a low-value group grew</td>\n<td>Break down by segment and compare the mix before and after</td>\n<td>PM and data science</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5. Abuse and fraud</td>\n<td>An implausible spike, one source or one signature, traffic that does not behave like users, or a drop caused by cleaning it up</td>\n<td>Look at the concentration (one IP range, one country, one device), the behavior after arrival (activation, retention) and the integrity team's actions</td>\n<td>Trust and safety, security</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6. A real change in behavior</td>\n<td>None of the above explains the size; changes are broad and gradual</td>\n<td>The residual: confirm with qualitative evidence (interviews, tickets)</td>\n<td>PM and research</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Order and why.</strong> Start with 1, because a measurement problem makes everything else wasted effort and is cheap to test. Then 2, because sudden changes more often follow something the company did than something users decided <code>Inferred</code>. Then 3, which is cheap to check against calendars and last year. Then 4 and 5, which need the segmentation of C13.5. Real behavior change, 6, is the explanation you reach by elimination and confirm with qualitative work, and it is the most expensive to act on, so it should be the last you claim.</p>\n<p><strong>Combinations.</strong> Changes often have more than one cause: for example, a holiday (3) and a release (2) together. The aim is to apportion the change among them, and the decomposition in C13.5 gives a way.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> The order matches the cost and the likelihood, so the first few checks remove most of the uncertainty. <strong>When not to follow it rigidly.</strong> If the first piece of evidence points strongly at one family (a release at the exact hour of the step), go there first and come back to the rest only if the size does not fit.</p>\n<span id=\"c13-c13-5-segmentation-the-cuts-that-matter\"></span>\n<h4>C13.5 Segmentation: the cuts that matter</h4>\n<p>To locate a change, break the metric down and see where it sits. FW-C13-04 gives the cuts in a useful order: <em>time</em> (the exact hour or day it began), <em>platform, device and app version</em>, <em>geography</em>, <em>acquisition channel</em>, <em>user type</em> (new or returning, tenure), <em>plan or tier</em>, <em>surface or feature</em>, and <em>funnel step</em> (C13.6).</p>\n<p><strong>Contribution analysis.</strong> The change in a total is the sum of the changes in its parts. If daily sign-ups fell by 500 and the channels fell by 50, 10, 300 and 140, then the third explains 60% of the drop and the fourth 28%. Report absolute contributions and relative changes together: a channel that fell 20% but is a small share may matter less than one that fell 4% and is large (EX-C13-03 works an example). A drop concentrated in one segment, a pattern consistent with the <a href=\"#gl-pareto-principle\">Pareto principle</a>, is a strong lead; a drop spread evenly across segments points toward a cause that touches everyone (a measurement change, a global release, an external event).</p>\n<p><strong>Rate effect and mix effect.</strong> A metric that is an average or a rate across groups, such as an overall success rate, is a mix of group rates weighted by group shares: overall = Σ share × rate. Its change splits exactly into two pieces:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the <strong>rate effect</strong>, Σ (old share) × (new rate − old rate): what happened within groups, at the old mix; and</li>\n<li>the <strong>mix effect</strong>, Σ (new share − old share) × (new rate): what happened because the composition changed, at the new rates.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A <strong>mix shift</strong> is a change in an overall metric caused by a change in the composition of users or traffic rather than a change in behavior within groups. It is the cause behind <a href=\"#gl-simpsons-paradox\">Simpson's paradox</a> (C12.7). Take a payment success rate (invented). Before: cards were 80% of payments with a 97% success rate, wallets 20% with 92%, so overall 0.8 × 97 + 0.2 × 92 = 96.0%. After: cards 70% with 94%, wallets 30% with 92%, overall 0.7 × 94 + 0.3 × 92 = 93.4%.</p>\n<p>The change is −2.6 points. Rate effect: 0.8 × (94 − 97) + 0.2 × (92 − 92) = −2.4. Mix effect: (0.7 − 0.8) × 94 + (0.3 − 0.2) × 92 = −9.4 + 9.2 = −0.2. Together, −2.6. So almost all of the fall is a real drop in the card success rate, and a little is the shift toward wallets. Had the card rate stayed at 97%, the mix effect alone would have been a small fall; had the shares stayed fixed, the rate effect would have been the whole story.</p>\n<p><strong>Nested cuts.</strong> After finding the segment, cut within it: web, then browser, then browser version. Stop when one cut explains most of the change (a rule of thumb: more than three quarters) and a specific, testable story is available.</p>\n<p><strong>Caution about many cuts.</strong> Slicing a metric 20 ways will always show an odd segment (C12.7). Cut in a planned order, expect some noise, require that a segment is large enough to matter and that its story matches the timing, and treat a surprise as a hypothesis to test, not a finding.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Segmentation converts a vague drop into a locus with a size. <strong>When not to use it.</strong> When the data is too thin to cut (a metric with a few hundred events a day), cutting makes noise, and qualitative evidence is better.</p>\n<span id=\"c13-c13-6-funnel-decomposition-and-cohort-comparison\"></span>\n<h4>C13.6 Funnel decomposition and cohort comparison</h4>\n<p>When the metric is the end of a journey (orders, activations, sign-ups), find the step that changed. A <a href=\"#gl-funnel\">funnel</a> breaks the journey into steps with a conversion rate at each (C05, FW-C05-07). Compare step by step, before and after. Because the steps multiply, a fall at one step shows up at the end: if conversion at one step fell from 70% to 55%, the end metric falls by about 21% from that step alone (0.55 ÷ 0.70 = 0.786), other steps equal. The step with the largest relative fall is where to look, and what happens at that step (a form, an email, a payment) suggests causes.</p>\n<p>Ask also <em>where the missing users went</em>: did they leave, or did they move to another path (a different surface, an unmeasured route)? A metric can fall because users shifted to something the metric does not count (a blind spot, Worked example 2 and IQ-C13-06).</p>\n<p><strong>Cohorts.</strong> Compare groups of users by when they arrived (C05, FW-C05-05). If new cohorts behave like old cohorts did at the same age, but there are fewer of them, the problem is acquisition. If recent cohorts are weaker at the same age, the problem is the quality of the users or the early experience. If old cohorts decay faster than before, something has changed for existing users. Each points to a different owner and fix.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> A funnel locates a change to a step and a cohort comparison separates new users from old, which narrows the causes sharply. <strong>When not to use it.</strong> When the metric has no steps (a count of daily visitors), use segments and time.</p>\n<span id=\"c13-c13-6a-getting-the-numbers-yourself-sql-for-segments-funnels\"></span>\n<h4>C13.6a Getting the numbers yourself: SQL for segments, funnels and cohorts</h4>\n<p>A diagnosis runs on numbers, and the quickest way to trust a number is to be able to pull it and check it yourself. <strong>SQL</strong> (structured query language) is the language most analytics databases use to read, filter and combine tables of data. <a href=\"#gl-sql\">SQL</a> lets a product manager do four things in this chapter: confirm a count (C13.3), cut a metric by segment (C13.5), build a funnel or a cohort table (C13.6) and split a change into rate and mix (FW-C13-04). The loop reports gathered for C33 do not mention SQL rounds for associate product manager roles <code>Inferred</code>; ask the recruiter whether a data or SQL exercise is part of a loop (C33.12). The skill is still worth having, because a PM who can check a number is harder to mislead and because it makes diagnosis answers concrete. Reading a query, and the fan-out trap, are taught in C21.4 (FW-C21-04, Worked example 2); this section is about writing the queries a diagnosis needs and checking them.</p>\n<p><strong>How far to go.</strong> Enough to write and read queries with counts, joins and groups; not enough to design a data warehouse. The examples use SQLite, which ships with Python, on synthetic data (invented); the scripts are in Appendix B, and every output below was produced by running the query. Date and string functions differ between databases, so check your dialect before copying a query.</p>\n<p><strong>Start from the grain.</strong> The <strong>grain</strong> of a table is what one row represents: one row per user, per event, per order, per user per day. Most wrong numbers come from not knowing the grain of the tables you join, or of the result you want. In the practice data, <code>users</code> has one row per user (<code>user_id</code>, <code>signup_date</code>, <code>channel</code>) and <code>events</code> has one row per user per event (<code>user_id</code>, <code>event</code>, <code>event_date</code>). &quot;How many users activated?&quot; has a result grain of one user, so the query must count users, not event rows.</p>\n<p><strong>Six patterns cover most PM questions.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>The point</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>How many people did X?</td>\n<td><code>COUNT(DISTINCT user_id)</code> with a <code>WHERE</code> on the event</td>\n<td>Count people, not rows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How many reach each funnel step?</td>\n<td>Conditional distinct counts in one pass</td>\n<td>One query, one population, comparable steps</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What is the rate by segment?</td>\n<td><code>users LEFT JOIN events</code> with the event condition in the <code>ON</code> clause, then <code>GROUP BY</code> segment</td>\n<td>Keep users who did not do X in the denominator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How do cohorts compare?</td>\n<td>Group users by signup week and flag whether each returned in a window</td>\n<td>Same age, same definition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How did this week compare with last?</td>\n<td>One row per week from one query</td>\n<td>The same definition every week</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What caused the change?</td>\n<td>Segment shares and rates by week, then the rate and mix split</td>\n<td>Locates the cause (FW-C13-04)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The first four, with their output on the practice data (5,000 users).</p>\n<p><em>A funnel in one pass:</em></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event='signup'    THEN user_id END) AS signed_up,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event='activated' THEN user_id END) AS activated,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event='purchased' THEN user_id END) AS purchased\nFROM events;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Output: 5,000 signed up; 2,574 activated; 530 purchased.</p>\n<p><em>A rate by segment:</em></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT u.channel,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT u.user_id) AS signups,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT a.user_id) AS activated,\n       ROUND(100.0 * COUNT(DISTINCT a.user_id) / COUNT(DISTINCT u.user_id), 1) AS activation_pct\nFROM users u\nLEFT JOIN events a ON a.user_id = u.user_id AND a.event = 'activated'\nGROUP BY u.channel;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Output: organic 2,248 signups, 1,208 activated, 53.7%; paid 1,745, 699, 40.1%; referral 1,007, 667, 66.2%.</p>\n<p><em>A cohort by signup week (a user counts as returned if the table has a <code>returned</code> event for them):</em></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT strftime('%Y-%W', u.signup_date) AS signup_week,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT u.user_id) AS signups,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT r.user_id) AS returned,\n       ROUND(100.0 * COUNT(DISTINCT r.user_id) / COUNT(DISTINCT u.user_id), 1) AS return_pct\nFROM users u\nLEFT JOIN events r ON r.user_id = u.user_id AND r.event = 'returned'\nGROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Output: week 2027-01, 1,698 signups, 275 returned, 16.2%; week 2027-02, 1,620, 206, 12.7%; week 2027-03, 1,682, 264, 15.7%. The data were built so that return depends only on the channel, so the lower middle week is chance: a few points of swing at this size is possible, which is why the noise band comes before an explanation (C13.3, EX-C13-02).</p>\n<p><strong>Four ways a query goes wrong.</strong> Each of the first three was run on the practice data.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Failure</th>\n<th>What it looks like</th>\n<th>Output</th>\n<th>The fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Fan-out (taught in C21.4)</td>\n<td><code>COUNT(*)</code> after joining <code>users</code> to <code>events</code> for the <code>signup</code> and <code>activated</code> events</td>\n<td>7,574 rows for 5,000 users</td>\n<td>Count distinct users, or aggregate the many-side to the grain of the one-side before joining</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>An inner join that removes the denominator</td>\n<td><code>users JOIN events</code> on the <code>activated</code> event, grouped by channel</td>\n<td>Each channel shows as many users as activated users (1,208 of 1,208 for organic): a 100% &quot;rate&quot;</td>\n<td><code>LEFT JOIN</code>, with the event condition in the <code>ON</code> clause</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Definition drift</td>\n<td>Two reports of &quot;week-1 return&quot; for the same users</td>\n<td>745 users counted as returned in days 7 to 13 after signup; 107 in days 1 to 7</td>\n<td>Write the numerator, denominator, window and population (FW-C05-01) and use one definition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A wrong population or window</td>\n<td>A cohort that has not had the full window yet; test or bot accounts; a day boundary in the wrong time zone</td>\n<td>Not shown</td>\n<td>State the as-of date, filter known test accounts and fix the time zone before comparing</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Check before you trust.</strong> A query that ran is not a query that is right. FW-C21-04 gives six checks: grain, join behavior, filters, nulls, time zone, and the total against a number you already trust (the practice data has 5,000 users). For diagnosis add two more. Check that the parts add to the whole and that every rate is between 0 and 100 and every count no larger than its denominator (on the practice data, the channel counts must sum to 5,000). And for a number that will drive a decision, get it a second way, such as a spreadsheet on a sample or a second query written differently, and read ten rows by eye.</p>\n<p><strong>Working with others.</strong> Say the definition first (numerator, denominator, window, population), then the grain and the join, then write the query in steps. For anything that will drive a decision, ask the data scientist who owns the tables to review it, and say which tables and filters you used. Use only data you have been given access to, and follow the privacy rules of the data (C05.9).</p>\n<p><strong>When not to write the query.</strong> When a trusted dashboard already answers the question, use it, and use SQL to check it or to answer what it cannot. The query is a way to check and to ask better questions, not a replacement for the data team's judgment.</p>\n<span id=\"c13-c13-7-forming-testing-and-ruling-out-hypotheses\"></span>\n<h4>C13.7 Forming, testing and ruling out hypotheses</h4>\n<p>A hypothesis (<a href=\"#gl-hypothesis\">hypothesis</a>, C03) is only useful if it predicts something you can check. FW-C13-06 is a ledger: for each candidate cause, write <em>what else would be true if it were the cause</em>, the cheapest check, the result, and the status.</p>\n<p>For example, take the hypothesis &quot;the new release broke sign-up on one browser.&quot; If true, the drop should begin at the release time, be concentrated in that browser and version, and appear in error logs for the sign-up call, while other browsers are flat. Each of these is a separate check. If the drop began a day before the release, or is flat for that browser, the hypothesis is ruled out cheaply.</p>\n<p><strong>Rules for the ledger.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Prefer checks that can refute.</em> Look for the observation that would show your favorite is wrong.</li>\n<li><em>Test timing, location and size</em> (C13.1).</li>\n<li><em>Rule out cheaply and early.</em> Ten minutes on a hypothesis that could take a day to confirm is a good trade.</li>\n<li><em>Allow partial causes.</em> Apportion the change: &quot;the holiday explains about 3 of the 4 points; the release about 1.&quot;</li>\n<li><em>Keep the leading hypothesis and the next one open.</em> Do not drop the second-best explanation until the first has explained timing, location and size.</li>\n<li><em>Know when to stop.</em> Stop when a cause explains the change on all three tests and you have a decision to make; the remainder, if small and within noise, can be left.</li>\n</ul>\n";
